⭐ 1. What is OAuth? (Simple Interview Explanation)

OAuth (Open Authorization) is an authorization protocol that allows users to log in or
give access to their data from another platform without sharing their password.

Example:

Login with Google
Login with GitHub
Login with Facebook
Login with LinkedIn
Google gives your app permission to access email/profile without giving your password.

One-line answer for interview:

“OAuth is an authorization protocol that lets users log in using third-party identity providers
(Google, GitHub, Facebook) without sharing their credentials. It ensures secure delegated access.”


⭐ 2. Why OAuth is used? (Benefits)
Feature	Why it is useful
No password sharing	Users never give their credentials to your app.
High security	Uses tokens, expires automatically, no password stored.
Faster onboarding	One-click login → better UX.
Verified identity	Google/GitHub guarantee user’s identity is genuine.
Reduces backend load	Your server does not manage passwords.
Industry standard	Used by Google, GitHub, Facebook, Microsoft etc

⭐ 3. How OAuth Works?
Step-by-step

1️⃣ User clicks “Login with Google”
2️⃣ Your app redirects user to Google’s OAuth consent page
3️⃣ User selects a Google account and gives permission
4️⃣ Google sends back an “Authorization Code” to your backend
5️⃣ Your backend exchanges this code for
✔ Access Token
✔ Refresh Token
6️⃣ Your backend verifies the user
7️⃣ Backend creates its own JWT (optional)
8️⃣ User is logged in

